so tell me how are those new year's  resolutions coming along  today i'm going to share with you a  better way to have your best year in  2022 in just four easy steps for the  last four years or so my husband and i  had a ritual at the end of the year  where we would sit down and reflect over  the past year by writing out an entire  page or pages of things that happen that  we're thankful for and sometimes that  list included even negative things that  actually helped us become better or  learn new lessons and then we would  follow that with creating our goals for  the next year and for us we divided that  into four different categories financial  spiritual social and health but this  year i wanted to try something different  you see what often ends up happening  when we set goals for the new year aka  resolutions is that we often start off  excited and motivated because we have  this fresh start this new beginning but  then the day-to-day challenges of life  just begin to weigh us down and little  by little we either forget about the  goals that we had set for ourselves to  begin with or we start losing that  motivation because we feel like our  goals were just too far too difficult to  achieve and we feel like we're never  really going to be able to accomplish  them or we lose momentum and just don't  take it so seriously as time progresses  because  after all we have the rest of the year  to work on it right  i've recently been inspired by the book  the 12 week work year and i realized  that even though i was always a goal  setter i've been going about it the  wrong way you see when i was a child i  was bullied and i didn't really feel  loved at home and so i felt like if i  achieved something worthy enough or did  something well enough then i would be  worthy of love and so setting goals just  kind of became natural for me and  actually when i was 21 my husband and i  got married i remember telling him that  by the time i was 27 we were gonna have  a paid for house and this and this and  this was how we were going to do it and  in many ways setting long-term goals has  been incredibly helpful but now i see  that i never really set short-term goals  for myself and this  hindered me in many aspects of my life  because i like many of us set these  wonderful goals when the new year began  and then somewhere along the ride they  just ended up shoved in a drawer along  with all my other unobtained goals  sometimes because the goals were just  not specific enough and other times  because they were just too high and  therefore impossible to obtain and other  times just because it was too much work  and i didn't feel like doing it the  12-week work year mentions several key  lessons but for me the biggest takeaway  is that instead of focusing on yearly  benchmarks like my husband and i had  been doing that instead much better  results can be obtained if you shorten  that time span from that of one year to  12 weeks or four quarters when i read  this this became like an aha moment for  me because in business the concept of a  four-quarter year was something that  i've heard of it was something that i  was familiar with but not something that  i had personally implemented in my life  and oddly enough right about the time  when i started to read the book was when  i started a 12-week planner completely  unintentionally it was something that i  actually picked up at a thrift store not  knowing that it was for 12 weeks only  but the benefits that i have noticed in  this first quarter of 12-week goal  planning is that when you have shorter  slots to achieve those goals each week  counts and each day is important and so  it makes you more aware of the fact that  you have only x number of days to  complete this task and it makes you less  likely to procrastinate or abandon ship  and if you actually achieve all of your  12 week goals and then some then you'll  gain motivation as you see your results  in this short amount of time and the  next go-around or the next quarter  you'll start off with even more  motivation even more momentum as you see  the progress that you've already made  and if you do fail and you don't achieve  the things that you had set for yourself  it's okay because guess what having a  horrible quarter does not mean that  you've had a horrible year you still  have another chance to try it again and  so if your results weren't as great as  you had hoped it's okay because you can  have that fresh start that the new year  brings all over again the next 12 weeks  i like to think of it as  four short sprints versus one long  marathon so you're my personal fitness  trainer and you tell me that i need to  run a mile i'd probably say okay i may  get a little short of breath but it's  for a short time i got this  but now if you tell me that i need to  run up a mountain without stopping  that changes things then i'm gonna look  at you like you're crazy that is never  gonna happen why is this it's because  now i'm not looking at this short  attainable goal i'm now looking at this  much longer task that now seems  impossible to obtain and the idea just  became much more daunting and looking  back my husband and i began to implement  the concept of short-term goals kind of  accidentally when we began to build this  house we had some contractor help for  things like foundation and electrical  but we did most of the work ourselves  and i mean we did plumbing completely  from rough to trim we installed flooring  we installed cabinets we installed  windows siding and the point that i'm  trying to make with all of this is that  because we were so physically exhausted  from this long project that seemed like  it had no end at night when we thought  about all the things that we still had  to cross off the list it made us lose  sleep it made us lose motivation it made  us feel like we were never going to  complete it and even if we had made  progress that day it felt like our  efforts were futile and it just kind of  emotionally made us lose that momentum  made us lose that motivation we thought  of the big picture it was highly  discouraging  have you ever given up on a project or a  goal because you felt like you weren't  making any progress if so what was it  let me know in the comments  i remember that when our mindset changed  was when i told my husband let's try  something different let's not focus on  the house being completed as the goal  let's focus on short-term goals and only  focus on those and so the goals began to  look a little bit more like this this  week we will finish rough plumbing and  lay tubs or this week we will finish  laying flooring so by doing this these  tasks now became things that we could  actually visualize as within sight and  so even though the overall goal didn't  change it was still to build the house  our energy was now focused on these  shorter term goals and this completely  changed our perspective because each  goal was something that we could  actually check off the list and feel  like we're making progress we're  actually achieving these things and so  if you've already forgotten about your  new year's resolutions or they're not  quite going like you had hoped it's okay  i want to encourage you to take your own  12-week challenge to do this first set  your overall goals for what you want to  accomplish in 12 weeks you can focus on  one area of your life or you can do like  i like to do and set four different  categories that is for me financial  spiritual social and health for example  if your goal is going to be financial  and you want to get out of debt then  you're going to actually want to pull  out of pen and paper and start to  calculate your income versus expenses so  that you can create a reasonable goal  once you've set your goals then you can  proceed to step two step two is actually  to take these 12-week goals and divide  them up into weekly segments so that you  can have an overview for the week ahead  each week so going back to the house  example you are now going to take your  longer 12-week goals and divide them up  into weekly chunks for example if your  goal was to read more what books will  you read when will you read them how  many chapters a day or each week will  you read  where and when will you read them when  you're going about setting either  long-term or short-term goals it's  important to make them as specific as  possible because this allows your brain  to actually have a plan to follow and a  vision to focus on as you're actually  putting these steps into practice this  will do wonders for helping you stay on  track so make them specific and  realistic because expecting that i'm  going to run a marathon in 12 weeks is  probably not realistic for me once  you've made your specific and realistic  goals it's time for step number three  which is to schedule them i like to do  this by taking some time on sundays to  first of all reflect on the previous  week with some gratitude journaling and  then follow that with planning for the  week to come i like to do this on my  physical planner because it's a one-stop  shop for me i can actually do my  gratitude journaling on it and my weekly  planning but you don't have to do the  journaling part if you don't want to you  can actually do this on a physical  calendar or even google calendar which i  personally love the point is just to  schedule these things so that they  actually get done so that just like you  schedule appointments with friends or  doctors and take them seriously you're  now scheduling appointments with  yourself because you are taking your  priorities seriously and as you go  through the week the last step step  number four would be to have weekly  evals with yourself so look back at the  week prior and ask yourself questions  like did i do as well as i had hoped  what could i have done differently what  did i do well what could i do the next  week to come  really be honest with yourself without  shaming it's not about reprimanding  yourself but rather analyzing how you  could approach it differently if it's  not working out and as you do this week  to week you're certain to learn more  about yourself and what works and what  doesn't work so that you can truly make  this your best year so there you have it  those are my four steps to creating a  better 2022 for yourself so to recap  number one would be to schedule 12-week  goals for yourself number two  two would be to divide them up into  weekly chunks number three would be to  actually schedule them so that they  happen and number four is to have weekly  evals with yourself so hopefully this is  helpful for you let me know if you have  tried out these shorter term goals and  what are you planning to do to make this  your best year i'd love to hear from you  in the comments and hear any suggestions  from you if you've made it this far  please give it a thumbs up and help  support my channel and subscribe so that  you won't miss when i post a new video  thank you so much for watching and i  can't wait to hear from you bye  you

